    {
      "question": "e-6754",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
      "old_sha256": "6aa3c7db6f59456da51d041366b12d187f7b1315e720e508fbb2722ed3eb1fd1",
      "new_sha256": "2f147b334b531d7679c1c2b1d4df52f6f25fea6e29cbddd7059b25526b8d6f52",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-6754",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "The range of $\\theta = \\arccos x$ is given by\n\n$$\n0 \\le \\theta  \\le \\pi.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta$ spans the first and second quadrants.\n\nSince $-\\dfrac{1}{2}$ is negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the second quadrant, where cosine is negative.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/assets/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the values in the second quadrant, we see that\n\n$$\n\\cos\\left( \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\right) = -\\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\arccos\\left(  -\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right) =\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}.\n$$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/example-6754.json",
          "file_sha256": "1046849a0250333f4ddc90c646133181eb24b87374281fc4a3c28c29f92f4bb1"
        }
      ],
      "basis": 1247,
      "reason": "Observed MA content supersedes the stored value",
      "action": "replace",
      "transaction_receipt": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/edb-import/commit.edn",
      "basis_after": 1248
