    {
      "question": "e-7220",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
      "old_sha256": "4d3c8db44a9f9a89995632369955bedccfe7fe915eff5c946dab47e8987d27f1",
      "new_sha256": "b895d779b6f91a641811cc7fe2bf939928f2070d92a84cf888e6022b526fe11f",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-7220",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "First, note that the range of $\\theta = \\arcsin x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac\\pi 2 \\le \\theta \\le \\dfrac\\pi 2.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/assets/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the values in the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left( \\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}\\right) = -\\dfrac {\\sqrt{3}} 2.\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta.$ To get an angle that's coterminal with $\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}$ and lies within the range of $\\theta,$ we subtract $2\\pi{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3} - 2\\pi = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\arcsin \\left(-\\dfrac {\\sqrt{3}} 2\\right) = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.\n$$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/example-7220.json",
          "file_sha256": "ddf59bf37fb261e092e472e14fd01d23a8bc9245a1f751991323279de7e7e790"
        }
      ],
